1
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:05,960
السلام عليكم ورحمة الله تعالى وبركاته

2
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:09,650
الحمد لله رب العالمين والصلاة والسلام على أشرف الأنبياء والمرسلين

3
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:11,900
وعلى آله وصحبه أجمعين, أما بعد

4
00:00:11,930 --> 00:00:15,370
So we've done some discussions on the near coherence.

5
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:18,470
I feel that that suffices for the purpose that I wanted to communicate to you.

6
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:21,460
That within surahs there's a progressive developing argument

7
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:24,010
that leads from the beginning to a conclusion.

8
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:25,670
The second thing I want to share with you,

9
00:00:25,670 --> 00:00:29,610
as far as coherence within surahs is concerned, is the concept of anchors.

10
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:32,040
So I'm gonna take a little bit of time and explain the

11
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,020
notion of anchors to you before we get into that subject matter.

12
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:35,460
Okay?

13
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,250
The term is something that I've kind of

14
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:39,820
made up.

15
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:41,350
I don't know what else to call it,

16
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:46,660
but there are words in the Qur'an in a surah, or themes, ideas inside of a surah

17
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,725
that are related to each other.

18
00:00:48,755 --> 00:00:50,090
So, for example, in Baqarah,

19
00:00:50,090 --> 00:00:56,450
you might find wording that has to do with buying or selling or trade or loan.

20
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:59,360
All of that revolves business transactions, right?

21
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:04,920
So you're going to find that kind of terminology occur in obvious contexts.

22
00:01:04,930 --> 00:01:05,830
Of course, there are

23
00:01:05,830 --> 00:01:09,280
aayaat about buying and selling and loaning and trading and all of that,

24
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:10,950
but you'll find that kind of language

25
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:16,150
also in "they sell the aayaat of Allah", "somebody who sold himself to please Allah",

26
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:19,970
or "they have purchased this life in exchange for the next" etcetera, etcetera.

27
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:24,560
So you're going to find that kind of verbage recur in unexpected places.

28
00:01:24,570 --> 00:01:26,350
And that's actually kind of like,

29
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:30,070
you know how there's a mental note - I remember back in the day,

30
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,050
there used to be these hot links in the text

31
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:33,880
- I don't know if we still do that, because it's a primitive thing -

32
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:36,420
but you, you can click it and it fast forwards to

33
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:39,190
the part of the page where that text is found.

34
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:42,150
But this kind of this kind of, this kind of an idea where, you know,

35
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:44,450
because of the wording used particularly

36
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:47,110
it reminds you of an aayah that you just came across,

37
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:51,140
or phrase that you just came across, or an image that you just came across.

38
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:55,460
And these are anchors that tie ideas together that otherwise, are unsuspecting.

39
00:01:56,140 --> 00:02:00,420
I want you - this is probably the easiest study of Nazam in the Qur'an,

40
00:02:00,430 --> 00:02:02,860
one aspect of coherence and how things are

41
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:05,500
tied together is how similar wording occurs.

42
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:07,660
So, for instance, um,

43
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:12,680
Surat al-Israa'. The word Qur'an itself is an anchor in Surat al-Israa'

44
00:02:12,684 --> 00:02:13,674
and Surat Bani Israa'eel.

45
00:02:13,690 --> 00:02:15,890
Or, for example, in Surat al-Dukhaan.

46
00:02:15,890 --> 00:02:19,050
If you read Surat al-Dukhaan on your own and you pay attention to the word "mubeen",

47
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:20,780
it'll come up over and over and over again,

48
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:23,900
in different contexts will be "dukhaanun mubeen", "rasoolun mubeen".

49
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:26,200
You know there'll be different "mubeen" contexts,

50
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,760
and it's kind of - it's almost as though there's

51
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:32,060
a - there's a demand to connect those dots together

52
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:33,760
and to look at them as a whole.

53
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:38,160
So there are going to be two examples of anchors that we're going to cover

54
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:39,200
One of them

55
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:40,940
I'll cover immediately now,

56
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:43,610
but the other one in shaa Allah when I - when I discuss

57
00:02:43,610 --> 00:02:46,960
the structural analysis of Surat al-Fath, surah number 48,

58
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:49,150
then the anchor will come up anyway.

59
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:51,280
It'll kind of become part of that discussion and you'll

60
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,040
see that anchor and I'll make mention of that.

61
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:53,730
So really,

62
00:02:53,730 --> 00:02:57,540
we're only doing two case studies of anchors for the purposes of this course.

63
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:01,560
So the anchor study that I want to do is a very simple one, but a very powerful one,

64
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:03,150
and that belongs to Surat Yaseen.

65
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,050
Okay, it's in Surat Yaseen.

66
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:09,760
And it's not like every surah, by the way, has one anchor.

67
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,810
It can have multiple strands of anchors.

68
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:17,170
There can be multiple terms that are recurring and are connected together.

69
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:17,650
Um,

70
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:21,230
just to give you some idea of some maybe more examples that are popping in

71
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:27,260
my head while, Iman and the heart, Iman and the heart are anchors in Baqarah.

72
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:31,610
So "qalb" comes up over and over again and becoming hard, in their hearts

73
00:03:31,610 --> 00:03:33,330
there's a disease, uh,

74
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:36,650
and you know so the qalb will keep coming up over and over again.

75
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:39,160
And so will the notion of Iman, of faith.

76
00:03:39,170 --> 00:03:40,420
By contrast,

77
00:03:40,430 --> 00:03:45,760
obedience and Islam are going to come up over and over again in Aali 'Imraan.

78
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:49,550
So here you have "Iman". And there you have "Islam".

79
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:51,840
Even though "Islam" occurs in both surahs.

80
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:55,550
But the frequency of Islam and the way it occurs in Aali 'Imraan is different.

81
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:57,600
And the way Iman occurs in Baraqah is different.

82
00:03:57,610 --> 00:04:01,710
Okay, so those would be kind of like - it's a simple way of Allah 'Azza wa Jall,

83
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:04,460
sort of developing the notion of Iman here

84
00:04:04,470 --> 00:04:06,660
and then furthering the notion of Islam there.

85
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:07,060
Okay,

86
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:09,020
in any case.

87
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:13,960
Surat Yaseen which we will come back to for multiple reasons today,

88
00:04:13,970 --> 00:04:16,269
includes a really interesting story,

89
00:04:16,269 --> 00:04:19,579
an account that is not found anywhere else in the Qur'an.

90
00:04:19,589 --> 00:04:24,950
It's a story of a nation in which, to which not just one but two,

91
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:28,760
and eventually three messengers were sent.

92
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:29,500
Okay,

93
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,890
so three messengers at the same time are

94
00:04:31,890 --> 00:04:34,040
delivering the message to one single nation.

95
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:34,550
Okay?

96
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:38,355
And they speak to the nation
فَعَزَّزْنَا بِثَالِثٍ

97
00:04:38,375 --> 00:04:40,265
إِذْ أَرْسَلْنَآ إِلَيْهِمُ ٱثْنَيْنِ

98
00:04:40,375 --> 00:04:43,085
فَكَذَّبُوهُمَا فَعَزَّزْنَا بِثَالِثٍ

99
00:04:43,105 --> 00:04:46,310
We sent two to them. They called both of them liars.

100
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:48,525
Then we reinforced them with the third one,

101
00:04:48,565 --> 00:04:50,295
فَقَالُوٓا۟ إِنَّآ إِلَيْكُم مُّرْسَلُونَ

102
00:04:50,355 --> 00:04:53,380
Then they all three of them said, "We're messengers sent to you"

103
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:55,160
and they were disregarded.

104
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:57,940
They were considered a bad omen, a curse, and people just, you know,

105
00:04:57,940 --> 00:04:58,910
disbelieved in them.

106
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:02,720
And then Allah describes a fourth. The fourth is not a messenger.

107
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,120
It's a person who came from another town.

108
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,360
The other end of the city there called "qariyah".

109
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:09,760
And this is this is Madinah

110
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:11,210
they

111
00:05:11,220 --> 00:05:13,250
وَجَآءَ مِنْ أَقْصَا ٱلْمَدِينَةِ رَجُلٌ يَسْعَىٰ

112
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:17,640
So from the end of the city, far end, a man comes running

113
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:19,350
And this man then speaks

114
00:05:19,470 --> 00:05:25,160
and he is recorded in, I think about 11 aayaat, are dedicated just to his speech.

115
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:31,160
So what's really peculiar about this story is that three messengers were talking

116
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:33,560
and there's just a couple of aayaat, one or two aayaat

117
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:35,050
for what they had to say.

118
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:38,120
And then this man came who was not a messenger,

119
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:39,660
and when he spoke,

120
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:41,980
his speech is the longest part of the story.

121
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:47,200
And you would think, just as far as typical protocol is concerned

122
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:49,860
when a messenger is there, a believer should be what?

123
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:53,660
Quiet. They are much more qualified to speak than I am.

124
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:55,060
So let them speak.

125
00:05:55,940 --> 00:06:01,430
And even more interesting that their account is less discussed

126
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:04,760
in the surah and Allah Himself discusses his account more,

127
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:07,800
and this is actually to highlight

128
00:06:08,210 --> 00:06:09,490
an interesting notion

129
00:06:09,990 --> 00:06:12,250
that everyone has their responsibilities.

130
00:06:12,710 --> 00:06:14,550
The messengers have their responsibility,

131
00:06:14,910 --> 00:06:15,960
they're doing their job.

132
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:19,720
But the one who believes in the messengers also has a responsibility,

133
00:06:19,730 --> 00:06:22,670
and he cannot put that off on even messengers.

134
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:24,260
That's a very powerful lesson,

135
00:06:24,350 --> 00:06:28,260
and as a matter of fact Allah acknowledges his contribution even more.

136
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:32,370
Because he didn't sit back and say, well, unlike any other nation,

137
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:33,710
they didn't just get one messenger,

138
00:06:33,710 --> 00:06:38,060
they got three. So I should just sit back.
There's enough people doing the right job

139
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:39,340
for me not to,

140
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:42,090
and I don't - I'm not even from this town, I'm from the far end of the city.

141
00:06:42,090 --> 00:06:44,350
These people don't even know me. Why would they even listen to me?

142
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:48,000
But he comes and he sees himself as someone who can contribute,

143
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,070
who feels an urge to deal with them, to talk to them, to convince them.

144
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:53,470
Maybe I'll bring them a point of view

145
00:06:53,470 --> 00:06:55,080
the messengers are not going to bring

146
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:57,020
because they've already rejected them as messengers.

147
00:06:57,030 --> 00:06:59,150
Maybe they'll see me as just one among themselves.

148
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:00,950
Maybe I can relate to them differently,

149
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:05,230
and he builds on all of that and communicates his message to them.

150
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:08,000
That's a pretty important lesson for Muslims to internalize today,

151
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,010
because we sometimes have this notion that there are good people in the community.

152
00:07:12,010 --> 00:07:14,490
Maybe there's an imam in the community, a scholar in the community,

153
00:07:14,490 --> 00:07:15,480
a teacher in the community.

154
00:07:15,490 --> 00:07:19,260
Somebody is there to do good. They're already doing their job. I don't have to.

155
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:22,190
There's other people that are already carrying out the work.

156
00:07:22,190 --> 00:07:25,260
That notion is shattered by this story.

157
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:28,940
It's completely destroyed by this story.
Whether you're by yourself - then you

158
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:32,380
then you're reminded of the Prophet's words
صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

159
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:33,590
بلغوا عني ولو آية

160
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:36,260
Communicate on my behalf, even if it's one aayah

161
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:43,290
and that that communication was not given to -
بلغوا عني ولو آية إذا قرأتمو التفاسير

162
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:47,490
وأخذتمو الإجازات في التجويد ثم بلغو عني

163
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:48,720
ولو آية

164
00:07:48,740 --> 00:07:51,930
Once finished reading all the tafaseer of an aayah and then do the

165
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:54,360
grammatical analysis and then get your ijazah in tajweed

166
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:55,550
and then you can talk about an aayah

167
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:58,660
He's talking to people that became Muslim the day before.

168
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:01,260
He's talking to Muslims that have been Muslim for 20 years.

169
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:04,660
He's talking to Muslims that took Shahadah that day,

170
00:08:05,540 --> 00:08:09,860
and he's telling all of them: go and communicate on my behalf,
even if it's a single aayah.

171
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:13,300
Is that a different sentiment than the way we carry Islam today?

172
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:14,060
It is,

173
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:17,960
It is. We feel like, well, first of all, other people are doing the job.

174
00:08:17,970 --> 00:08:19,390
Second of all, we're not qualified.

175
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:22,660
Who's asking you to interpret Islamic law?

176
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:28,230
The aayaat of the Qur'an are not just there for complex theological or legal discourse.

177
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:32,130
The aayaat of the Qur'an are there for a reminder, for counsel, for softening hearts.

178
00:08:32,150 --> 00:08:33,540
Softening hearts - that's something that

179
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:34,860
anyone can do.

180
00:08:35,409 --> 00:08:38,159
That's not just something that very highly qualified are supposed to do.

181
00:08:38,539 --> 00:08:42,429
And what are what are one man's qualifications compared to three messengers?

182
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:43,159
Nothing.

183
00:08:44,039 --> 00:08:45,850
How is he? What position is he to speak?

184
00:08:45,850 --> 00:08:47,440
But when it comes to reminder and giving

185
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:49,810
counsel in giving advice and something of benefit,

186
00:08:49,820 --> 00:08:52,260
everybody is equally qualified and actually responsible.

187
00:08:52,740 --> 00:08:54,560
Everybody's responsible. Okay,

188
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:57,010
now the anchor that I want to refer to -

189
00:08:57,010 --> 00:08:58,960
I wanted to give you this background to the story -

190
00:09:00,140 --> 00:09:03,670
then Allah switches the subject and starts talking about signs of creation.

191
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:04,790
Story is done.

192
00:09:04,810 --> 00:09:09,790
وَءَايَةٌ لَّهُمُ ٱلْأَرْضُ ٱلْمَيْتَةُ أَحْيَيْنَـٰهَا وَأَخْرَجْنَا مِنْهَا حَبًّا فَمِنْهُ يَأْكُلُونَ

193
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:12,240
He starts talking about the earth that was dead, and you know,

194
00:09:12,250 --> 00:09:14,110
fruits come out of it and you eat from it.

195
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:15,560
Then there's a particular

196
00:09:16,340 --> 00:09:18,760
aayah that uses the word "yanbaghee".

197
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:21,760
Allah 'Azza wa Jall says

198
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:25,740
وَٱلشَّمْسُ تَجْرِى لِمُسْتَقَرٍّ لَّهَا

199
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:32,840
The sun flows according to a station and a schedule that is dedicated for it.

200
00:09:32,850 --> 00:09:34,920
ذَٰلِكَ تَقْدِيرُ ٱلْعَزِيزِ ٱلْعَلِيمِ

201
00:09:34,930 --> 00:09:36,290
That is the decree,

202
00:09:36,290 --> 00:09:39,000
and that is the calculation, of the One who has

203
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,620
the ultimate authority and the One who knows everything.

204
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:43,490
وَٱلْقَمَرَ قَدَّرْنَـٰهُ مَنَازِلَ

205
00:09:43,490 --> 00:09:46,140
And the moon We break it down into stages

206
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:48,810
حَتَّىٰ عَادَ كَٱلْعُرْجُونِ ٱلْقَدِيمِ

207
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:54,690
until it turns into an old - it looks like an old dried up, uh, you know,

208
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:56,760
a part of a palm tree,

209
00:09:57,340 --> 00:09:59,950
لَا ٱلشَّمْسُ يَنۢبَغِى لَهَآ أَن تُدْرِكَ ٱلْقَمَرَ

210
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:03,060
It's not appropriate for the sun

211
00:10:03,540 --> 00:10:06,170
to go to the place of the moon.

212
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:08,730
The sun has its own orbit.

213
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:13,760
The moon has its own orbit. It's not right
for the sun to go and take over the moon.

214
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:15,860
وَلَا ٱلَّيْلُ سَابِقُ ٱلنَّهَارِ

215
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:19,900
and nor is it right for the night to get ahead of the day.

216
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:20,910
وَكُلٌّ فِى فَلَكٍ

217
00:10:20,950 --> 00:10:23,470
And all of them have their own circular orbits

218
00:10:23,499 --> 00:10:24,190
يَسْبَحُونَ

219
00:10:24,229 --> 00:10:25,170
in which they are swimming.

220
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:28,260
They all have their own paths, their own trajectories that they need to take.

221
00:10:28,850 --> 00:10:31,560
The word that I wanted to highlight as an anchor is "yanbaghee".

222
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:34,770
The word "yanbaghee" occurs only twice in this surah.

223
00:10:34,930 --> 00:10:36,960
"Yanbaghee" means for something to be appropriate,

224
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:41,540
and it's not appropriate for the sun to take over the moon.

225
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:42,560
That's the aayah.

226
00:10:43,040 --> 00:10:47,130
By the end of the surah, Allah says about the Prophet
صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

227
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:51,180
وَمَا عَلَّمْنَـٰهُ ٱلشِّعْرَ وَمَا يَنۢبَغِى لَهُۥٓ

228
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:53,500
We didn't teach him poetry at all,

229
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:56,250
nor is it something appropriate for him.

230
00:10:57,180 --> 00:10:58,450
Okay?
وَمَا يَنۢبَغِى لَهُۥٓ

231
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:04,460
So the word "yanbaghee" occurs for two entities. On the one hand it occurs for the sun

232
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:06,760
and the other it occurs. For who?

233
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:09,360
The Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ
Okay.

234
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:14,420
Interestingly enough, in other places in the Qur'an, Allah 'Azza wa Jall says

235
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:18,150
وَدَاعِيًا إِلَى ٱللَّهِ بِإِذْنِهِۦ وَسِرَاجًا مُّنِيرًا

236
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:20,850
Allah has sent the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

237
00:11:20,850 --> 00:11:24,030
I have sent you as a caller to Allah by his leave,

238
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:26,360
and I have sent you as a brilliant lamp,

239
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:28,350
as a brilliant lamp.

240
00:11:28,740 --> 00:11:31,500
The term used for the Prophet, to describe the Prophet, is what?

241
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:33,680
سِرَاجًا مُّنِيرًا
Okay,

242
00:11:34,740 --> 00:11:36,180
The Allah 'Azza wa Jall says,

243
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:39,750
تَبَارَكَ ٱلَّذِى جَعَلَ فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ بُرُوجًا

244
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:41,560
وَجَعَلَ فِيهَا

245
00:11:41,960 --> 00:11:44,850
سِرَٰجًا وَقَمَرًا مُّنِيرًا

246
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:50,360
How blessed is the One who placed constellations in the sky and put a lamp in it

247
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:53,160
and an illuminating moon.

248
00:11:54,440 --> 00:11:55,760
Well, what's the lamp?

249
00:11:57,160 --> 00:12:02,560
What's the lamp? When Allah says Allah put constellations in the sky and a brilliant lamp? The sun

250
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:06,400
In Surat Yaseen "yanbaghee"was used for the sun,

251
00:12:06,410 --> 00:12:09,190
and "yanbaghee" was used for the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ. 

252
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:14,600
In other places in the Qur'an, the Prophet is called a lamp, and so is the sun, also called a lamp.

253
00:12:14,610 --> 00:12:17,350
There seems to be a correlation between the sun and the Prophet
صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

254
00:12:17,350 --> 00:12:21,450
in the Qur'an. Do you understand? And nowhere is that more visible

255
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:22,860
than in Surat Yaseen,

256
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:26,250
when even the same verb is associated with both of these entities,

257
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:29,160
the sun and Rasool Allah
صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

258
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:30,360
Now,

259
00:12:31,140 --> 00:12:33,890
before I talk to you about the sun and the moon and

260
00:12:33,890 --> 00:12:37,780
the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ being described as the sun,
I went somewhere else.

261
00:12:37,790 --> 00:12:41,580
I described to you the story of three messengers and one man.

262
00:12:41,590 --> 00:12:43,760
It seemed that all of that was unrelated.

263
00:12:44,140 --> 00:12:45,160
But wait a second.

264
00:12:45,540 --> 00:12:48,560
What is the relationship between the sun and the moon? Do they have the same orbit?

265
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:50,560
No.

266
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:53,790
What is the relationship between a messenger and his followers?

267
00:12:53,790 --> 00:12:55,400
Do they have the same responsibilities?

268
00:12:56,340 --> 00:12:58,170
Does the Messenger's responsibility

269
00:12:58,180 --> 00:13:01,860
absolve the follower from his responsibilities?

270
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:04,230
Just because the messengers are doing dawah,

271
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:07,270
should the follower who came afterwards not do his own work?

272
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,750
No, they both have their own paths that they must take.

273
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:13,650
Just like the sun has its own path, and the moon

274
00:13:14,140 --> 00:13:15,660
has its own path. Now

275
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:17,550
the sun has its own light,

276
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:20,260
but the moon - does it have its own light?

277
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:25,750
No, the moon simply reflects the light of the sun.

278
00:13:26,940 --> 00:13:30,760
The moon is dependent for its light from the sun.

279
00:13:30,770 --> 00:13:34,860
Do you see a parallel between the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ and his followers

280
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:37,860
as a parallel between the sun and the moon?

281
00:13:38,740 --> 00:13:41,950
Because the followers of the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ, whatever light they have

282
00:13:42,340 --> 00:13:47,209
is only a reflection of what was given to them by the Sun, صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

283
00:13:47,249 --> 00:13:48,160
You get it?

284
00:13:48,340 --> 00:13:50,650
That's the lamp that illuminated them.

285
00:13:51,140 --> 00:13:56,150
So we are to the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ, what the moon is to the sun.
Now,

286
00:13:56,840 --> 00:13:58,160
when the sun is there,

287
00:13:58,940 --> 00:13:59,860
it's brilliant,

288
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:02,550
but eventually the sun goes away,

289
00:14:03,740 --> 00:14:05,580
and when the sun goes away, there is darkness.

290
00:14:06,130 --> 00:14:07,460
Except there's one thing left.

291
00:14:07,940 --> 00:14:11,830
It's not quite like the lamp that the sun is, but there's still a lamp in the sky.

292
00:14:11,830 --> 00:14:12,760
And what lamp is that?

293
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:15,770
In the absence of the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

294
00:14:15,770 --> 00:14:18,020
The only reflection of the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

295
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:20,060
left in the world will be his followers.

296
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:27,760
We are a glimpse of the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ to the world.
We are ambassadors, like

297
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:31,260
Like 'Umar ibn al-Khattaab says: أنا رسول رسول الله
صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

298
00:14:32,140 --> 00:14:35,600
The existence of the moon and it's shining the light of the sun,

299
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:39,420
even if you can't see the sun, is actually an indication that we represent,

300
00:14:39,430 --> 00:14:41,500
we reflect the teachings of the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

301
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:43,560
just like the moon reflects the light of the sun.

302
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:45,660
Take it a step further.

303
00:14:46,340 --> 00:14:48,760
The sun doesn't necessarily have phases,

304
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:51,360
but the moon -

305
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:52,650
What happens to it?

306
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:56,860
It has a full moon. It's brilliant.

307
00:14:57,440 --> 00:14:59,180
And then it starts weakening, weakening,

308
00:14:59,180 --> 00:15:02,260
weakening to the point where it's
عَادَ كَٱلْعُرْجُونِ ٱلْقَدِيمِ

309
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:05,550
Then it starts strengthening again and rises again,

310
00:15:05,550 --> 00:15:09,180
and it's back to full moon again. And then it gets weak again.

311
00:15:09,190 --> 00:15:12,520
Sometimes what happens to the ummah of a prophet is that the

312
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:15,970
light that they reflect from their messenger becomes so so so,

313
00:15:15,970 --> 00:15:18,350
so weak you can barely see anything.

314
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:20,460
It's barely visible,

315
00:15:20,940 --> 00:15:23,640
and then they're back up on a decline - or on a

316
00:15:23,650 --> 00:15:26,270
on a rise until they become a full moon again.

317
00:15:26,270 --> 00:15:28,860
But is even the full rise of an ummah permanent?

318
00:15:29,440 --> 00:15:32,660
No. Then decline can come to them again, and they can rise again.

319
00:15:32,670 --> 00:15:35,762
The ummah, like a believer, will go through phases. The Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

320
00:15:35,770 --> 00:15:36,520
Allah says about him,

321
00:15:36,550 --> 00:15:38,200
لِنُثَبِّتَ بِهِۦ فُؤَادَكَ

322
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:42,110
So that we can firm your heart. The Prophet's heart صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ is always constant.

323
00:15:42,120 --> 00:15:47,780
Is a believer's heart always constant? Is a believer's heart always reflecting the light of the Prophet's teaching the same way?

324
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:50,680
No, it's actually going through phases,

325
00:15:50,840 --> 00:15:54,050
just like the moon goes through phases. So by that

326
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:56,210
tiny little anchor "yanbaghee",

327
00:15:56,450 --> 00:16:00,260
some profound lessons are taught about our relationship with the

328
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:02,010
Messenger of Allah صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ

329
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:06,772
and how, no matter how - you can't even compare what the Prophet صَلَّى ٱللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ did,

330
00:16:06,802 --> 00:16:10,192
the light that he shines on the world, to what we're gonna do.

331
00:16:10,242 --> 00:16:13,040
There's no comparison between the light that the sun produces and

332
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:15,470
what it does to the earth, and what the moon produces.

333
00:16:15,470 --> 00:16:16,760
There's still darkness on the earth.

334
00:16:17,340 --> 00:16:22,560
But for those - that still doesn't take the responsibility of the moon away.

335
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:24,530
It has its own course to play.

336
00:16:24,530 --> 00:16:27,460
It has its own role to play, no matter how imperfect it gets.

337
00:16:27,470 --> 00:16:30,950
Even if this this little light left, you still have to shine it.

338
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:34,560
Even if it's not perfect, even if it's not a full moon.

339
00:16:34,570 --> 00:16:38,480
So that would be one of my favorite anchors in the Qur'an,

340
00:16:38,490 --> 00:16:40,850
because it's just mind blowing to me.

341
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:43,460
Just through the word "yanbaghee", okay?

